FILES AND DIRECTORY WORKFLOW - READ BEFORE MODIFYING

PicoNIX has ROM programs under /bin, read-only /man, dynamic /proc,
devices /dev, and small RAM-backed writable /tmp and /etc.
This is not a persistent Linux disk filesystem.

READ-ONLY RECONNAISSANCE
  pwd
  ls -la /
  ls -la /tmp
  tree -L 2 /man
  cat /man/README
  cat -n /tmp/demo.py
  grep socket /man/network
  find /tmp -name demo.py
  hexdump /tmp/demo.py 0 64

WRITE/DELETE ONLY WHEN ASKED
  write_file(path='/tmp/demo.py', content='print(123)\n')
  python /tmp/demo.py
  cp /tmp/demo.py /tmp/copy.py
  rm /tmp/copy.py
MCP write_file is restricted to /tmp and ASCII, RAM file <=2048
bytes. Avoid overwriting the user's current test scripts.

REDIRECTION AND PIPES
  echo hello > /tmp/message
  cat /tmp/message
  echo hello | grep he
  cut -d, -f1,4 /tmp/data.csv
  echo more >> /tmp/message
Redirection opens a real file; many /dev nodes provide an infinite
stream. Do NOT use unbounded cat on /dev/gpio/N or /dev/mem.
'grep' matches literal substrings and has no -i, regex engine,
or GNU-style options. Lines longer than 128 bytes are skipped.

ROM MANUAL
  ls /man
  cat /man/limits
  cat /man/languages/c
  grep write /man/syscalls
Use get_manual(topic) if a tool call needs one page returned directly.
The /man pages are ROM data: they cannot be edited using write_file.
